What is vector indexing?
This is an important interview topic.
Suppose we have one million vectors.
Without an index, an exact search might calculate the distance between the query vector and every stored vector.
That can become computationally expensive.
A vector index helps speed up retrieval.
Important indexing techniques
HNSW organizes vectors into a graph with multiple layers.
- Higher layers help navigate large distances.
- Lower layers provide more detailed searching.
- The algorithm follows connections to find nearby vectors

B. IVF divides vectors into clusters.
- Each cluster has a centroid.
- During search, the system identifies promising clusters.
- It searches within selected clusters rather than all vectors.

C. Product Quantization (PQ)

PQ compresses vector representations into smaller codes.
It can reduce memory requirements and improve search efficiency, with a potential accuracy trade-off.

Interview answer: Vector indexing improves search efficiency by organizing vectors so the system does not always need to perform exhaustive comparisons.

