13. Interview questions and answers
Q1. What is a vector database?

A vector database is a system designed to store and search numerical vector representations using similarity measurements. In GenAI applications, it is commonly used to retrieve semantically relevant documents.

Q2. Why do we need a vector database in RAG?

RAG applications need to retrieve relevant document chunks based on user queries. A vector database stores document embeddings and supports similarity search to find relevant information efficiently.

Q3. What is the difference between FAISS and ChromaDB?

FAISS is a vector similarity-search library that provides indexing and retrieval capabilities. ChromaDB is a vector database that supports storing vectors, document text, IDs and metadata together.

Q4. What is HNSW?

HNSW stands for Hierarchical Navigable Small World. It is a graph-based approximate nearest-neighbor indexing algorithm used to perform efficient vector searches.

Q5. What is the difference between a vector and a vector index?

A vector is a numerical representation of data. A vector index is a data structure that organizes vectors to make similarity searches more efficient.

Q6. Why do we normalize embeddings?

Normalization scales vectors to unit length. When both vectors are normalized, their dot product equals cosine similarity, simplifying similarity calculations.

Q7. What happens if the embedding model changes?

The new model may produce vectors in a different vector space or with different dimensions. Existing document vectors should generally be regenerated using the new model before searching with new query embeddings.

Q8. Can FAISS store document text?

FAISS primarily stores and searches vectors. We generally maintain a separate mapping or document store to associate returned vector positions with the original text and metadata.

Q9. Why would we use OpenSearch instead of FAISS?

OpenSearch is useful when the application needs a broader search platform, including keyword search, vector search, metadata filtering and hybrid retrieval. FAISS focuses primarily on vector similarity search.

Q10. How would you handle outdated banking policies in a vector database?

I would maintain document IDs, version numbers, effective dates and status metadata. During ingestion, I would update or remove superseded records and filter retrieval to approved, currently valid policies.
I would also enforce access controls and maintain audit records.


